# SPEC: 65_symbol_clearance
# static-check: off
# Requires: driver
# NEEDS: dynload
# NOT MEASURABLE as long as spec/29_2 §4.3 is missing — and that is not down to this probe:
#   `sys.bundle` takes the NAME of a function the host compiled itself.  So
#   every bundle from a file carries a name the host ALREADY HAS, and lands in the
#   REPLACE branch (`clearance-replace`) instead of the CREATE branch.  Measured 2026-10-17:
#   `fresh_helper` was rejected exactly that way.
#   The guard `clearance-create` is built and stands correctly fail-closed; it only becomes
#   reachable once a host can accept foreign code with a FOREIGN name.  Whoever builds §4.3
#   redeems this probe — and F65-27 along with it, which hinges on the same point.
# TIER: T1
# CONSTRUCT: spec/65 F65-26 — `create_level` governs what gets into the HOST's table, and
#            that can only happen in the SHARED context (F65-31).  Here the host grants the
#            right: policy (7, 7, 0) means clearance 7, may create, may NOT replace.
# COVERS: the policy travels as a triple with `sys.exec` (F65-36), in the order of the
#         F65-26 table; with `create_level` granted a NEW name is admitted.
#         The counterpart is neg/n20 — the same situation without the right, but `clearance-create`.
#         LIMIT: that the new name then also becomes CALLABLE is spec/29_2 §4.3
#         ("dynamic loading not yet supported") and not this chapter's business.  What is checked
#         here is the PERMISSION, not the loading — otherwise the probe would claim more than it
#         can measure.
# EXPECT: exit 0.

- def:
    name: fresh_helper
    return: i32
    body:
      # 0, so that a non-zero result can come ONLY from the denial:
      # `sys.exec` returns the return value of the `start_call`.
      - return: [0]

- def:
    name: main
    return: i32
    body:
      - decl: [pkg, Code, { call: [sys.bundle, "fresh_helper", { context: false }] }]
      - decl: [rcx, i32, { call: [sys.exec, pkg, 7, 7, 0] }]
      - if:
          condition: { neq: [rcx, 0] }
          then:
            - call: [log.error, "creating with create_level 7 returned ${rcx}, expected 0"]
            - return: [1]
      - call: [log.info, "PASS 11 create_level granted"]
      - return: [0]
